Stop libevent's poll backend spinning on dead descriptors on Haiku.

Haiku has neither epoll nor kqueue, so libevent falls back to poll(), and
poll() reports POLLNVAL, POLLERR and POLLHUP whether or not they were asked
for. Two consequences, and this port hits both.

First: poll_dispatch() turns revents into libevent events with

	if (what & (POLLHUP|POLLERR))
		what |= POLLIN|POLLOUT;
	...
	if (res == 0)
		continue;

POLLNVAL is not in that set, so a watched descriptor that has been closed
produces res == 0 and is skipped, and nothing ever tells its owner.

Second, and this is what actually spins: the poll array keeps slots whose
events have all been cleared. Such a slot has neither POLLIN nor POLLOUT, so
poll_dispatch() finds no r_ev and no w_ev to dispatch to -- but poll() still
reports POLLNVAL on it if the descriptor is gone. poll() therefore returns
immediately, dispatches nothing, and is called again. It never blocks again
for the life of the process.

Measured on the VAIO (2026-09-20), loading https://x.com/i/flow/login: the
poll set holds 15 descriptors, six of them (0, 55, 60, 61, 63, 64) come back
POLLNVAL, and strace catches 8357 poll() calls in six seconds, every one of
them returning those same six. `top` shows NetworkService at 49.6% of a
two-CPU machine with 4748 ms of each 5000 ms interval in the kernel -- in
poll()'s own select_fd / create_sem_etc / delete_sem_internal machinery, which
is expensive on Haiku because every call builds and tears down a semaphore and
a select entry per descriptor. Half the machine is gone from ten seconds into
the page load onwards. On a 1.33 GHz Atom that is the difference between a
usable browser and one where typing a character into a text field does not
show up inside a minute. about:blank never triggers it; it takes x.com's
connection churn.

Note that fd 0 is among them, which is the same descriptor the 2026-09-15
`close(0)` bug closed underneath the browser. Something on Haiku closes
descriptors this process still owns; the event loop has to survive that rather
than melt.

One Haiku detail matters for the first part: its poll() ORs
POLLERR|POLLHUP|POLLNVAL into the caller's `events` field, so after the first
call every slot has a non-zero `events` and "is anything still registered
here?" has to be asked as "is POLLIN or POLLOUT set?". A first attempt at this
patch tested `events != 0` and therefore did nothing at all.

The fix is in two parts. Slots that nothing is watching are dropped before
each poll: a slot any watcher actually holds always has POLLIN or POLLOUT, so
this cannot drop a live one, and if the descriptor comes back poll_add()
builds a fresh slot. And POLLNVAL now joins POLLHUP and POLLERR so that a
watched descriptor which does go invalid reaches its owner, which closes it
and unregisters it the ordinary way.

Both are guarded on Haiku because that is where it was measured; upstream
libevent has the same gap but nothing on Linux closes Chromium's descriptors
behind its back.

--- a/base/third_party/libevent/poll.c
+++ b/base/third_party/libevent/poll.c
@@ -143,6 +143,47 @@
 	if (tv != NULL)
 		msec = tv->tv_sec * 1000 + (tv->tv_usec + 999) / 1000;
 
+#if defined(__HAIKU__)
+	/* Drop slots nothing is watching any more.
+	 *
+	 * poll() reports POLLNVAL, POLLERR and POLLHUP whether or not they
+	 * were asked for. A slot whose events have all been cleared has
+	 * neither POLLIN nor POLLOUT, so there is no r_ev or w_ev below to
+	 * dispatch to -- but if its descriptor has been closed it still comes
+	 * back POLLNVAL on every call, and this loop then spins for the life
+	 * of the process. A slot a watcher actually holds always has POLLIN
+	 * or POLLOUT, so nothing live is dropped here, and poll_add() builds
+	 * a fresh slot if the descriptor comes back.
+	 */
+	for (i = pop->nfds - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
+		struct pollfd *dead = &pop->event_set[i];
+
+		/* Haiku's poll() ORs POLLERR|POLLHUP|POLLNVAL into the
+		 * caller's events field, so "events != 0" is true of every
+		 * slot after the first call. Ask what this loop actually
+		 * means: is anyone watching for readability or writability?
+		 */
+		if (dead->events & (POLLIN|POLLOUT))
+			continue;
+
+		pop->idxplus1_by_fd[dead->fd] = 0;
+		--pop->nfds;
+		if (i != pop->nfds) {
+			/*
+			 * Shift the last pollfd down, as poll_del() does. The
+			 * entry moved in comes from above i, which this loop
+			 * has already looked at and kept.
+			 */
+			memcpy(&pop->event_set[i],
+			       &pop->event_set[pop->nfds],
+			       sizeof(struct pollfd));
+			pop->event_r_back[i] = pop->event_r_back[pop->nfds];
+			pop->event_w_back[i] = pop->event_w_back[pop->nfds];
+			pop->idxplus1_by_fd[pop->event_set[i].fd] = i + 1;
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	nfds = pop->nfds;
 	res = poll(pop->event_set, nfds, msec);
 
@@ -177,8 +218,16 @@
 		res = 0;
 
 		/* If the file gets closed notify */
+#if defined(__HAIKU__)
+		/* ... and if it is already gone, say so too, so that whoever
+		 * owns it finds out and unregisters it.
+		 */
+		if (what & (POLLHUP|POLLERR|POLLNVAL))
+			what |= POLLIN|POLLOUT;
+#else
 		if (what & (POLLHUP|POLLERR))
 			what |= POLLIN|POLLOUT;
+#endif
 		if (what & POLLIN) {
 			res |= EV_READ;
 			r_ev = pop->event_r_back[i];
